\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Overview of the VGGT architecture, taken from~\blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {wang2025vggt}. The input images are split into patch tokens by DINO and augmented with camera tokens. $L = 24$ blocks of frame-wise and global self-attention process the tokens of all images jointly, after which the camera head predicts the camera parameters and the DPT head the dense outputs.}}{8}{figure.caption.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces A graph with three connected components (dashed). The nodes $u$ and $w$ are not adjacent, but they belong to the same component because they are connected by a path via $v$.}}{10}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces RANSAC fitting a circle to 2D points with outliers. Three points (minimal sample) determine a candidate circle, and the points within the tolerance band form its consensus set. The least-squares fit to all points (dashed) is pulled toward the outliers.}}{14}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces DBSCAN and agglomerative clustering with complete linkage on three compact groups, two of which are connected by a chain of points. DBSCAN links these two groups into a single cluster (left; gray: noise). Complete linkage keeps all three groups apart, and the chain forms a small cluster of its own (right).}}{15}{figure.caption.9}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Overview of the counting pipeline. VGGT predicts a point map for every view, and SAM3 segments the apples as instance masks. After outlier removal, the masks are matched between all pairs of views and aggregated into multi-view instances, which are checked against a sphere as apple model. In the refined pipeline, instances that are too large are re-associated with stricter thresholds (dashed). The centers of the accepted spheres are clustered to obtain the count.}}{18}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Running example used throughout this chapter: five views of seven apples on a table, partially separated by a cardboard partition.}}{18}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Sampled pixel correspondences from view~1 (left) to view~4 (right) of the running example, colored by instance. Apples hidden by the partition in one of the views receive hardly any correspondences.}}{21}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Mask graph of the running example. Each column corresponds to a view, each node to an instance mask (labeled with its in-view identifier), and each row to one connected component. The edges represent accepted matches.}}{23}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Sphere fitting in the running example. (a) The seven instances in the reconstructed scene, one color per instance. (b) Their fitted spheres, shown as points. All spheres satisfy the radius criterion.}}{24}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Clustering in the running example, with the correspondences between views~1 and~3 switched off. (a) The two instances of one apple in the reconstructed scene. (b) Their points with the centers of their fitted spheres (red). (c) The fitted spheres nearly coincide, so the clustering merges both instances.}}{25}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Iterative refinement in the running example, with deliberately permissive thresholds. (a) An instance of the initial graph (gray) that contains masks of two apples on both sides of the partition. (b) The stricter association splits it into two instances with one apple each. (c) After clustering, the split-off part of the left apple is merged with the remaining masks of that apple.}}{28}{figure.caption.17}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Example views of the two trees. (a) Synthetic tree, rendered in Blender. (b) tree\_02 from the FruitNeRF dataset~\blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {meyer2024fruitnerf}. In both, each apple covers only a small part of the image.}}{30}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Groups of masks on the synthetic tree, shown by their apple points and colored by their correctness (green: exactly one apple, not split; orange: one apple, split over several groups; red: several apples; gray: discarded). (a) Components of the initial graph, in which only a few apples are recovered correctly. (b) Final clusters of the refined pipeline: more apples are recovered correctly, but split and merge errors remain.}}{32}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Correspondence precision against recall, with the distance threshold $\tau $ as curve parameter, for the synthetic tree (point map head and depth with camera) and the table scene. Stars: threshold used by the pipeline.}}{33}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Apple points of the synthetic tree before outlier removal, one color per ground-truth apple, within the reconstructed scene. (a) Point map head: most points of each apple lie in a dense core, surrounded by sparse, isolated outliers of the kind removed by the outlier filter (Section~\ref {sec:method_outlier}). (b) Depth maps unprojected with the predicted cameras: fewer isolated outliers, but the points of each apple scatter more widely.}}{34}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces Final clusters on tree\_02 with all thresholds scaled by a factor of $2$, one color per cluster.}}{35}{figure.caption.26}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
